-- Lazy stream-producing combinator (WI-064): map.
--
-- Unlike the eager consumers on `Stream` (collect / takeN / foldLeft /
-- foldRight / find, which fold the stream down to a value), `map` PRODUCES a
-- new stream, so it is a lazy carrier: a small entity that wraps the source
-- COLLECTION plus the transform and PROVIDES Stream by defining `splitFirst` over
-- the source. The carrier's output element param is named `T` and its effect
-- `E` — matching Stream's own param names so the `provides Stream[T, E]`
-- provision conforms (a divergent name like `Dst`/`Eff` loses the
-- binding in the cross-sort provider view; cf. `List provides Stream[T, {}]`).
--
-- `filter` is the sibling lazy carrier (FilteredStream, below). A kept element
-- is returned VERBATIM (unlike `map`'s `fn(h)`, whose output type comes from the
-- transform's return), so the source head's element type must thread through
-- `splitFirst`'s destructured `Pair` (WI-356 / WI-357), AND a dropped element is
-- skipped by RECURSING on the reconstructed carrier — whose incurred effect
-- must thread too (WI-413). Both work off the sort's own params; the skip goes
-- through `Stream.splitFirst` rather than a static self-call, because since WI-590
-- the reconstructed carrier wraps the source's TAIL and so has a different
-- `Source` than the receiver (see the operation's own note).
sort anthill.prelude.MappedStream
  import anthill.prelude.{Stream, Iterable, Function, Option, Pair, Modify}
  import anthill.prelude.Option.{some, none}
  import anthill.prelude.Pair.{pair}

  -- WI-590: the SOURCE's own sort, KEPT IN THE TYPE. The field used to be typed
  -- `Stream[Src, ES]`, which ERASED which carrier the source was — and with it every
  -- property of that carrier, finiteness included. That erasure is the only reason a
  -- separate finite twin carrier had to exist: nothing could condition on the source.
  -- Threading `Source` gives a WITNESS the handle it needs to say "a mapped stream is
  -- finite WHEN its source is" (`MappedStreamFinite`, finite_combinators.anthill).
  --
  -- The field is typed on the `Iterable` SPEC VIEW rather than left a bare `Source`
  -- because `Src` and `ES` appear in no other field: a bare param would leave both
  -- unbound at every construction and the provided `Stream[E = {ES, EF}]` row would leak.
  -- `Iterable` and not `Stream` because Stream is SELF-RECEIVER and cannot name WHICH
  -- carrier it is, while everything mappable provides Iterable.
  sort Source = ?
  sort Src = ?
  sort T = ?
  -- WI-441 decoupled rows: the SOURCE's observation row and the TRANSFORM's
  -- own row are independent; the provided Stream's row is their UNION
  -- (both are paid on consumption — splitFirst peels the source AND runs fn).
  effects ES = ?
  effects EF = ?

  -- The field's arrow row is the PLAIN `{EF}`: the `-Modify[x]` lacks lives
  -- on the PUBLIC signatures (map / Iterable.map), where the WI-440 call-site
  -- check enforces it — an entity FIELD has no registered binder places
  -- (the WI-341 arrow-binder context is operation-param scoped).
  requires Iterable[C = Source, Element = Src, E = ES]

  entity mapped(source: Iterable[C = Source, Element = Src, E = ES], fn: (Src) -> T @ {EF})

  provides Stream[T = T, E = {ES, EF}]

  -- WI-492: a mapped stream IS iterable, derived TRANSITIVELY — `MappedStream
  -- provides Stream` (above) and `Stream provides Iterable` (stream.anthill),
  -- so the engine resolves `Iterable` ops on a `mapped(…)` value through Stream
  -- (its `iterator(s) = s` is the genuine impl). No explicit `provides Iterable`
  -- + identity `iterator` is needed here: in the `xs.map(f).filter(p)` chain the
  -- `.filter` hop's own `iterator(c)` over this lazy carrier resolves to
  -- `Stream.iterator` via transitive provision.

  -- Stream primitive for a mapped stream: peel the source's head, apply the
  -- transform, and re-wrap the source's tail so the result stays lazy. The
  -- output element type comes from `fn`'s return `B = T`, so it threads even
  -- though the bound `h`'s own type does not (cf. the filter note above).
  --
  -- WI-590: the peel is `Stream.splitFirst(Iterable.iterator(src))`, both QUALIFIED —
  -- `src` is an abstract `Iterable` view, so it must be turned into a Stream first, and an
  -- unqualified call has no concrete receiver carrier for WI-411's redirect to key on. On a
  -- `Stream` source `iterator` is the identity, and the re-wrapped TAIL is itself a Stream,
  -- so every peel after the first takes that path.
  --
  -- The tail is a bare `Stream`, exactly what `Stream.splitFirst` promises. It also states
  -- the truth: the tail wraps the SOURCE's tail, so its `Source` is that tail's sort, NOT
  -- this carrier's — a `MappedStream[Source = Source]` tail would make the witness read
  -- finiteness off the wrong sort.
  operation splitFirst(m: MappedStream) -> Option[Pair[A = T, B = Stream[T = T, E = {ES, EF}]]] effects {ES, EF} =
    match m
      case mapped(src, fn) ->
        match Stream.splitFirst(Iterable.iterator(src))
          case none() -> none
          case some(pair(h, rest)) -> some(pair(fn(h), mapped(rest, fn)))

  -- `map` ON A MAPPED STREAM — the chaining hop of `xs.map(f).map(g)`.
  --
  -- WI-20260829-X13YV. This was a STATIC CONSTRUCTOR over a bare Stream
  -- (`map[S, Dst, EffS, EffP](s: Stream[S, EffS], f) -> Stream[Dst, {EffS, EffP}]`),
  -- and being declared HERE is what made the chain unreachable. Dot dispatch resolves a
  -- member on the receiver's OWN sort BEFORE the specs it provides — the
  -- `own_op.or_else(find_spec_op_for_provided_sort)` ladder in `typing.rs` — so `.map` on
  -- a mapped stream landed on this declaration, while `.filter` on the SAME value fell
  -- through to `FiniteCollection.filter` because this sort has no `filter` member. Same
  -- chain, different member name, different route; the mixed chains are what localized it.
  --
  -- Landing here was fatal twice over. `EffS` did not ground from
  -- `provides Stream[T = T, E = {ES, EF}]` (WI-594's gap 2), so the chain did not load;
  -- and the return ERASED the source to a bare `Stream` — the erasure WI-590 removed the
  -- finite twin carriers to be rid of — so even grounded it would have yielded a value no
  -- finiteness witness can read, and `xs.map(f).map(g).size()` would still be refused.
  --
  -- THE RETURN REUSES THE INPUT TYPE: the receiver is this carrier, and the result names
  -- it as its own `Source`. So `MappedStreamFinite` keys on it and RECURSES — a two-hop
  -- chain is finite exactly when the ORIGINAL carrier is. Driven both ways in
  -- `x13yv_map_map_chain_test`: over a `List` the chain collects and evaluates; over an
  -- infinite `Nats` this hop still LOADS (chaining a lazy stream is always sound) and only
  -- the CONSUMPTION is refused, naming `FiniteCollection[C = Nats]`.
  --
  -- NOT `FiniteCollection.map` UNDER ANOTHER NAME: that one carries `requires
  -- FiniteCollection`, so it cannot serve a mapped stream over an infinite source. This
  -- one can, which is what the `Nats` row measures.
  --
  -- GENERAL OVER ANY ITERABLE SOURCE (WI-20260829-70XVH). X13YV had to narrow the input to
  -- THIS carrier: the general form needs an op-level `requires` on a FREE operation to
  -- ground the element type from the argument's own provision, which WI-599 delivered for
  -- the SPEC-METHOD shape only and explicitly excluded for this one (measured then: `Dst`
  -- does not ground, and the row leaks). 70XVH closed that exclusion, so the input is `Sc`
  -- again and a bare `List` is accepted — driven in `wi599_carrier_arg_provision_test::
  -- the_stdlib_combinators_are_general_over_any_iterable_source`.
  --
  -- THE ROUTE IS UNCHANGED and so is the finiteness reading: `.map` on a mapped stream still
  -- lands here (the dot ladder reads the receiver's OWN sort first), `Source = Sc` is still
  -- the receiver's own type, so `MappedStreamFinite` still recurses on it, and a `.map` over
  -- an infinite source still loads while only its CONSUMPTION is refused. `Iterable.map`
  -- remains the erasing spelling.
  operation map[Sc, S, Dst, EffS, EffP](s: Sc, f: (x: S) -> Dst @ {EffP, -Modify[x]})
    -> MappedStream[Source = Sc, Src = S, T = Dst, ES = EffS, EF = EffP]
    requires Iterable[C = Sc, Element = S, E = EffS] =
    mapped(s, f)
end

-- Lazy stream-producing combinator (WI-410): filter.
--
-- The sibling of `map`. A filtered stream wraps the source plus a predicate and
-- PROVIDES Stream by defining `splitFirst`: advance the source until a KEPT
-- element (`pred(h)` true), returning it + the filtered tail; SKIP a dropped
-- element by RECURSING on the reconstructed `filtered(rest, pred)`.
--
-- Two threadings the eager `find` / lazy `map` did not exercise together (WI-413):
--   * the kept head `h` is returned VERBATIM, so the source element type must
--     thread through `splitFirst`'s destructured `Pair` (WI-356 / WI-357), and
--   * the SKIP RECURSION re-incurs the carrier's effect, which must close to the
--     declared row rather than leak an unresolved `??_`.
-- Both work off the sort's OWN params (`T`, `ES`, `EF`): the destructure threads
-- the element and the source row in through the field-type deep-walk, and the
-- reconstructed carrier's row closes to `{ES, EF}` by ordinary unification. The
-- skip is `Stream.splitFirst(filtered(rest, pred))` and NOT a static self-call —
-- since WI-590 the reconstruction wraps the SOURCE'S TAIL, whose sort differs from
-- the receiver's `Source`, so the value no longer has the receiver's own type;
-- dispatching through `Stream` lands back here on that value's own instance.
sort anthill.prelude.FilteredStream
  import anthill.prelude.{Stream, Iterable, Function, Option, Pair, Bool, Modify}
  import anthill.prelude.Option.{some, none}
  import anthill.prelude.Pair.{pair}

  -- WI-590: the source's own sort, kept in the type — cf. the MappedStream note.
  sort Source = ?
  sort T = ?
  -- WI-441 decoupled rows (cf. MappedStream): source row + pred row, the
  -- provided Stream's row is their union.
  effects ES = ?
  effects EF = ?

  -- Plain `{EF}` row — cf. the `mapped` field note (lacks on public sigs).
  requires Iterable[C = Source, Element = T, E = ES]

  entity filtered(source: Iterable[C = Source, Element = T, E = ES], pred: (T) -> Bool @ {EF})

  provides Stream[T = T, E = {ES, EF}]

  -- WI-492: a filtered stream IS iterable, derived TRANSITIVELY (cf.
  -- MappedStream) — `FilteredStream provides Stream provides Iterable`, so
  -- `iterator` on a `filtered(…)` value resolves to `Stream.iterator` through
  -- transitive provision; no explicit `provides Iterable` + identity `iterator`.

  -- WI-590: the source peel is qualified (cf. MappedStream). The SKIP recursion is
  -- `Stream.splitFirst(filtered(rest, pred))`, a spec dispatch where it used to be a static
  -- self-call: the reconstructed carrier wraps the source's TAIL, so its `Source` differs
  -- and the value no longer has the receiver's own type. Dispatching through `Stream` lands
  -- back here on that value's own instance, which drops the next element and threads the
  -- carrier effect (WI-413).
  operation splitFirst(f: FilteredStream) -> Option[Pair[A = T, B = Stream[T = T, E = {ES, EF}]]] effects {ES, EF} =
    match f
      case filtered(src, pred) ->
        match Stream.splitFirst(Iterable.iterator(src))
          case none() -> none
          case some(pair(h, rest)) ->
            if pred(h) then some(pair(h, filtered(rest, pred)))
            else Stream.splitFirst(filtered(rest, pred))

  -- The twin of `MappedStream.map` — X13YV's repair and 70XVH's generalization both; read
  -- that operation's note for the mechanism. The element is kept VERBATIM by a filter, so
  -- the result's `T` IS the source's element where `map`'s becomes `Dst`.
  operation filter[Sc, S, EffS, EffP](s: Sc, p: (x: S) -> Bool @ {EffP, -Modify[x]})
    -> FilteredStream[Source = Sc, T = S, ES = EffS, EF = EffP]
    requires Iterable[C = Sc, Element = S, E = EffS] =
    filtered(s, p)
end
